{"id":"51714968-3572-44f5-9427-98c5c150d1c0","object":"chat.completion","created":1784826510,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case[\\\"payload\\\"]\\n    nodes = payload[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    node_map = {node[\\\"id\\\"]: node for node in nodes}\\n    children = {node[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for node in nodes}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        pid = node.get(\\\"parent\\\")\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(node[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    retained = set(node[\\\"id\\\"] for node in nodes)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if node[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        if node[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_children = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_children:\\n            continue\\n        remaining_symbols = set()\\n        for rid in retained:\\n            if rid != nid:\\n                remaining_symbols.add(node_map[rid][\\\"symbol\\\"])\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(remaining_symbols):\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"retained\\\": sorted_retained, \\\"removal_order\\\": removal_order}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to interpret the task. We have to implement `solve(case)` that returns a JSON-serializable object. The case payload has nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" - presumably we need to reduce the tree by removing nodes that are removable, but preserving required symbols and grammar validity? The fixture shows examples. The description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But we don't have an actual grammar or interestingness oracle. We have only the nodes and required_symbols. The objective seems to be: given a tree, we want to find a subset of nodes to keep such that all required symbols are present, and we want to remove as many nodes as possible? Or it's about order of removal? The description of the function: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to produce two lists: retained node IDs sorted, and the order in which nodes were removed. The process: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" \"Use node ID as stable tie break at equal depth.\" \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we need to simulate a reduction algorithm: iterate over nodes in some order (deepest first, ties by node ID?), and for each node that is removable? Not all nodes are removable. The nodes have a \"removable\" field. Only removable nodes can be removed. But we also have to check that removing it doesn't remove any required symbol. Required symbols are the symbols that must be present in the final tree. The root must always be kept. Also note: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That means: we cannot remove a node if it has children that are retained (i.e., not removed). So the removal must be from leaves upward? Actually, if we remove a node, its subtree is gone. So we can only remove a node if none of its descendants are kept. But the statement \"a node with retained children\" suggests we cannot remove a node if any of its children are still in the retained set. So we must process bottom-up: only leaves (nodes whose children are all removed) can be considered for removal. And we must also ensure that all required symbols are still present after removal.\n\nThe algorithm: Start with all nodes retained. Then iterate over nodes in order: deepest first (higher depth first), and for nodes with equal depth, sort by node ID (likely alphanumeric or numeric? The IDs are strings like \"root\", \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. We need stable tie break. Use the ID string itself as tie-breaker? \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we sort nodes by depth descending, then by ID ascending (or default string order). Then for each node that is removable (and not root, and required_symbol does not include its symbol? Actually \"never remove ... a required symbol\" means nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols list cannot be removed. Also the root cannot be removed. Also we cannot remove a node that has retained children. So before removal, we check: if node is not root, node.removable is True, node.symbol not in required_symbols, and all its children (immediate? Actually \"retained children\" means any descendant that is still in the retained set? But usually children are direct. The condition likely means if any of its direct children are still present (not removed), then cannot remove because that would orphan them? Actually if you remove a node, its children are also removed. So you cannot remove a node if you want to keep any of its descendants. So we need to ensure that all descendants that are still retained are none. So we can only remove a node if its entire subtree (except itself) has already been removed. So that aligns with bottom-up. So we can simulate: initially all nodes are retained. For each node in bottom-up order, we check if removal is allowed. If allowed, we remove it (mark as removed). We also record the removal order. At the end, retained IDs are those not removed. Sort them (probably alphabetically? The description says \"sorted retained IDs\" - likely sort by ID). Also return the removal order (list of IDs in order they were removed). We should also consider \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So after removal, we must check that all required symbols still appear among retained nodes. This check must be done before actually removing. So for each candidate removal, we simulate the removal and check if the set of symbols among retained nodes still contains all required_symbols. If yes, then perform removal. If not, skip. Also note: \"Use only quantities present in the case payload.\" So we don't have any grammar validation beyond that? We have no grammar rules. So we assume the tree is already grammar-valid? The reduction must be grammar-valid deletion. But without grammar, we cannot validate replacements. The fixture only gives these nodes. So maybe the only constraints are the ones given: removable flag, required symbols, etc.\n\nWe need to output something like: {\"retained\": [...], \"removal_order\": [...]}. But the problem statement says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So likely a dict with two keys. However, the instruction says \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" So we need to decide the exact field names. Based on the task scaffold: \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, `implementation`. Its value must be Python source defining `solve(case)`.\" So we output JSON with implementation key, whose value is the Python source code as a string. That Python source code must define solve(case) that returns a JSON-serializable object. So the solve function itself returns the result. The result should be a dict with keys \"retained\" and \"removal_order\"? Or maybe \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\"? The description says \"sorted retained IDs\" and \"the actual removal order\". So likely \"retained\" (list of strings) and \"removal_order\" (list of strings). But we must ensure consistency.\n\nLet's examine the fixture: it shows cases with nodes. The required_symbols include \"root\" and \"s1\". In the first case: nodes: root (depth0, removable false, utility 1.0, symbol root), n1 (parent root, depth1, symbol s1, removable false), n2 (depth1, s2, removable true), n3 (depth1, s0, removable false), n4 (depth2, parent n2, s1, removable true), n5 (depth2, parent n3, s2, removable false), n6 (depth2, parent n1, s0, removable true). Required symbols: root and s1. So we need to keep at least one node with symbol root (root itself) and at least one node with symbol s1. Nodes with s1: n1 (depth1, not removable) and n4 (depth2, removable). So n4 is removable, but if we remove n4, we still have n1 with s1, so allowed. n6 is removable (symbol s0, not required). n2 is removable (symbol s2). n3 is not removable. n5 is not removable. So we can potentially remove n2, n4, n6. We need to process deepest first: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. But n5 is not removable. n4 is removable but we need to check if its children? n4 has no children (no node has parent n4). So it's a leaf. Removing n4: does it remove any required symbol? It has symbol s1, but there is another s1 (n1), so yes, required symbols remain. Also, n4's parent is n2. If we later remove n2, that's fine. So n4 can be removed. Depth2 nodes sorted by ID? IDs: n4, n5, n6. So order: n4, n5, n6. n4 removable, remove. Record removal_order: n4. Then mark n4 as removed. Next n5 not removable, skip. n6 removable, check: n6 has no children. Its symbol s0 not required. Removing n6? But after removing n4, still all required. n6's parent is n1. So n6 can be removed. Remove n6. Now depth1 nodes: n1 (not removable), n2 (removable), n3 (not removable). n2 is removable, but it has a child? n2's child is n4 which is already removed, so no retained children. n2's symbol s2 not required. Removing n2 still leaves root and s1 (n1). So remove n2. Then depth0 root not removable. So retained nodes: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted retained IDs: maybe alphabetical: n1, n3, n5, root. Removal order: n4, n6, n2. That seems plausible.\n\nBut what about the check \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That we did. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" We didn't remove nodes with retained children (n2 had child n4 but n4 was already removed, so no retained children). Good.\n\nThus algorithm: \n1. Build a map id->node dict from nodes list.\n2. Build children map: for each node, children = list of node IDs whose parent == this node's ID.\n3. Determine initial set of retained IDs = all node IDs.\n4. Determine required_symbols set.\n5. Determine all nodes sorted by depth descending, then by ID (string comparison) ascending. (Since depth is integer, descending means deeper first.)\n6. Iterate over sorted nodes:\n   - skip if node.id == \"root\" (or parent is null?) Actually root's parent is null, and removable false. But check condition.\n   - skip if not node.removable.\n   - skip if node.symbol in required_symbols.\n   - check if node has any retained children: i.e., any child ID that is still in retained set. If yes, skip.\n   - simulate removal: check if after removing this node, the set of symbols from all remaining retained nodes still covers required_symbols. That is, compute symbols of retained (excluding this node) and see if all required symbols present.\n   - If valid, then remove: remove node.id from retained set. Append node.id to removal_order list.\n7. After loop, retained set contains IDs not removed. Sort retained list by ID (string sorting, ascending). Return {\"retained\": sorted_retained, \"removal_order\": removal_order}.\n\nThat should work for the given cases. There is no randomness. Also need to handle cases where removal is infeasible? But we just skip. The output will be whatever results. Possibly if no nodes can be removed, removal_order empty, retained all IDs sorted.\n\nWe must ensure we don't mutate case. We'll create local data structures. Use only standard library.\n\nNow produce the Python source string. Need to escape for JSON. Write a function solve(case). The case is a dict with key \"payload\" which contains \"nodes\" list and \"required_symbols\" list. We'll extract them. We'll assume the structure is exactly as given. We'll raise ValueError if something is missing? The problem says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" But we can simply check for presence. Also \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case.\" But what is infeasible? If required_symbols cannot be satisfied? But that's not the case here. We'll just proceed and if something goes wrong, we could raise ValueError. But the requirement says \"instead of silently repairing it.\" So we should validate that all nodes have necessary fields. But we can assume they do.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case[\"payload\"]\n    nodes = payload[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\"required_symbols\"])\n    # Build id->node\n    node_map = {node[\"id\"]: node for node in nodes}\n    # Build children map\n    children = {node[\"id\"]: [] for node in nodes}\n    for node in nodes:\n        pid = node.get(\"parent\")\n        if pid is not None and pid in children:\n            children[pid].append(node[\"id\"])\n    retained = set(node[\"id\"] for node in nodes)\n    # Validate root exists and is not removable? Not necessary.\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    removal_order = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        # Skip root, non-removable, required symbol\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:  # root\n            continue\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check retained children (immediate or any descendant? Only immediate children that are retained)\n        # According to condition: \"a node with retained children\" - likely direct children.\n        has_retained_children = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_children:\n            continue\n        # Simulate removal: compute remaining symbols\n        # We need to check if all required symbols are present after removal.\n        # We can compute symbols of retained (excluding nid) and see if required_symbols subset.\n        # But this is O(n) each time. Could optimize by precomputing symbol presence, but n is small.\n        remaining_symbols = set()\n        for rid in retained:\n            if rid != nid:\n                remaining_symbols.add(node_map[rid][\"symbol\"])\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(remaining_symbols):\n            continue\n        # Remove\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"retained\": sorted_retained, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n\nThat should work.\n\nBut need to be careful: root's parent is null, but in the data parent is null, so check node[\"parent\"] is None. Also note that nodes have \"id\" field. Sorting by ID string should be fine.\n\nNow encode as JSON string. We'll create a Python string with proper escaping. Use json.dumps on the string? Actually we need to output a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source code as a string. So we need to write:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n\nBut the entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object with that key. So we need to produce the JSON string. We can manually construct the string with escaped newlines and quotes. Or we can use Python's json.dumps to serialize a dict. But since the instruction says \"Encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\" So we can write the Python code as a normal string in our response, but the response must